# Goal: collect a bounded, read-only memory diagnosis for one exact systemd
# service. Replace the unit with the service under investigation.
#
# The report connects unit state, the service cgroup, process RSS, cgroup
# memory counters, OOM counters, and a short journal window. It does not claim
# to identify the root cause of an incident: cgroup membership is not semantic
# ownership, counters are observations, and the journal may be incomplete.
#
# Environment: Linux, a running systemd manager, readable cgroup v2 state, and
# permission to read the selected unit and its user/system journal. This file
# does not start, stop, signal, throttle, or limit the service.
#
# Bash comparison:
#   systemctl show --no-pager --property=Id,LoadState,ActiveState,SubState \
#     api.service
#   systemctl show --no-pager --property=ControlGroup,MemoryCurrent,MemoryMax \
#     api.service
#   journalctl --no-pager --unit api.service --since=-15m --lines=40
#
# Role of yesh: keep the unit identity, bounded observations, and failure
# boundary visible; systemd, cgroup v2, /proc, and journald remain the sources.

service status systemd-journald.service
service resources systemd-journald.service
journal systemd-journald.service since 15m lines 40
